SIGNAL = r"""{\sffamily\bfseries\small\color{Deep}The first multi-day controlled human exposure to near-ambient PM$_{2.5}$: three consecutive days at 35\,$\mu$g/m$^3$ --- the US 24-hour standard --- left FEV$_1$ unchanged on day 1 but 1.9\,\% lower on day 2 (95\,\% CI 0.7--3.1) and 1.8\,\% lower on day 3, with the deficit still present 20 hours after exposure ended.}\\[1.4mm]
\footnotesize
US EPA, \textit{Environmental Health Perspectives}; randomised, double-blind crossover, 17 healthy volunteers, 4 h/day with intermittent exercise, clean-air sessions at least two weeks apart. Exercise output fell 2.0\,W on day 2 and 2.4\,W on day 3.
\textbf{Where it is weak:} n=17 healthy young adults; decrements of about 2\,\% sit near spirometric reproducibility, and the sex-modification claims are underpowered.
\textbf{Why it matters here:} the effect accumulates across days at a concentration that is legal for a single day. Sensor-network alerting built on single-day exceedance flags misses exactly this; multi-day running means are the defensible public metric."""

